# A character's background, on four axes: where they live, how they were raised, who they belong
# to, what they believe. Ported from SierraBay12's culture descriptor.
#
# Descriptions are Bay's own text where Bay wrote it in English, and translated from the Sierra
# mods where they wrote it in Russian. Paragraph breaks are Bay's <br><br>.

## Homeworld — Sol

ember-homeworld-mars = Mars
ember-homeworld-mars-details = Capital: Olympus. Distance from Sol: 1.5 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-mars-desc =
    Mars, the current capital of the SCG and known by its nickname the Red Planet, is one of the most populous centres of humanity. Since Jimmy Gideon founded the first colony many years ago, Mars has been an important player on the human stage. Nobody breathes outside and nobody ever will, so the population lives either in huge domed cities and agri-domes or within a vast tunnel network connecting large underground cities. The rich surface against the poor tunnellers is the primary divide on the planet, and Mars is rife with dissent, crime and continuous unrest, especially underground.{"\u000A\u000A"}The people of Mars vary, from the downtrodden tunnellers to the cosmopolitan urbanites of Olympus to the hardy farmers of the agri-domes, but they all share a strong sense of Martian pride. Their history has not been an easy one: as the new primary home of humanity they have seen great struggles over the centuries, from revolutions to political upheaval to regular rioting. Martians consider themselves tougher than most, with a fierce pride that clashes not only with other planets but with other parts of Mars.

ember-homeworld-earth = Earth
ember-homeworld-earth-details = Capital: none. Distance from Sol: 1 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-earth-desc =
    Earth, home of humankind, has long been in a state of recovery following centuries of pollution and exploitation. Today much of it exists as a nature reserve dedicated to preserving the history of humanity, and the humans who remain live mostly in large urban zones: huge planned cities of arcologies built around eco-friendliness. No longer the political power of humanity, Earth is content to be something of a backwater and a tourist destination for those looking to discover their heritage.{"\u000A\u000A"}Earth has no capital, and that is not an oversight. None of its peoples has held a colony in space for a very long time, but hundreds of them still insist on their own identity, and not one will accept anybody else speaking for it. Inside the SCG, Earth exists as a confederation nobody wanted and nobody can dissolve: every question is settled by the agreement of all, and the agreement of all is the absence of a decision. Earthers are not troubled by this. They are wealthier than most humans on average, remarkably ordinary, much as they have been for hundreds of years, and generally content with their lot.

ember-homeworld-luna = Luna
ember-homeworld-luna-details = Capital: Selene. Distance from Sol: 1 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-luna-desc =
    Luna, the moon of Earth, was long a goal for humanity and a symbol of space exploration. Today it is one of the oldest homes of humanity, and the home of the rich, the powerful and the corporate. It is divided into interconnected domes scattered across the surface, each containing its own city known as a prefecture, and its culture varies from prefecture to prefecture. Its capital, Selene, is a centre of culture, the arts, trade and politics, and it is from here that the people of Luna take the name of their language, Selenian. Despite its status Luna is not without its problems, though with most of the population upper and upper-middle class these tend to manifest as political machinations and elaborate plots. Corporations still flock to Luna to build a prefecture and establish a headquarters there as a symbol of success.{"\u000A\u000A"}Lunites are often considered the old money of humanity, many with traceable lineages to the first settlers, to old Earth dynasties and to heavy corporate ties. Most are rich, cultured and refined. Not all: much of the city of New Vegas lives on the poverty line, and its people often hold a quiet resentment towards the rest of Luna.

ember-homeworld-venus = Venus
ember-homeworld-venus-details = Capital: Central Administration Station. Distance from Sol: 0.7 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-venus-desc =
    Venus cannot be lived on, and that took a while to establish. The planet was hung about with dozens of orbital stations on the assumption that a way would be found sooner or later: first built for the cities to come, then for work rotations, then simply because the construction was already under way. No way was found — or rather several were, and every one of them ran into the cost of lifting anything off the surface, which made mining or manufacturing down there pointless. The project was quietly wound up. Nobody demolished the stations: they were already there, they held air, and people were living in them.{"\u000A\u000A"}So they stayed. More people live in Venus orbit today than on some colonies, and they live well enough: old habitation modules have been rebuilt into everything from hotels to orchards, and docking hubs sized for a freight volume that never arrived took tourists and transit instead. People still go down, to service the automated extraction platforms that just about pay for themselves. Venusians divide along exactly that line: those born in orbit who have never set foot on the planet they live under, and those who go down for the bonus.


ember-homeworld-ceres = Ceres
ember-homeworld-ceres-details = Capital: Government Zone, Ceres Station. Distance from Sol: 2.7 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-ceres-desc =
    Ceres Station, officially the Ceres Mining and Habitation Outpost, was historically a significant mining facility for humanity. That importance has diminished greatly over the past few decades, and today Ceres is a sprawling, populous facility covering much of its namesake. Operated primarily by Falco Designs, one of the SCG's premier shipbuilders, it is home to a multitude of drydocks and service centres, and it is the primary stop for vessels entering the Sol system. It is often said that anything worth anything passes through Ceres, which has given it a reputation for a thriving black market.{"\u000A\u000A"}Commonly called Belters, the people of Ceres today work mostly in shipbuilding, manufacturing, vessel services and hospitality. They regard themselves as adaptable, proud members of the SCG and some of the best engineers humanity has to offer.

ember-homeworld-kuiper-belt = Kuiper, Belt
ember-homeworld-kuiper-belt-details = Capital: Kuiper Administrative Zone, Pluto. Distance from Sol: 45 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-kuiper-belt-desc =
    The Kuiper Special Economic Zone was once at the forefront of human exploration, back when the road outward ran through it. Once the gateways existed the road stopped running through it at all, and the Zone's usefulness collapsed inside a generation. It is treated now as an economic afterthought with little more than a defensive station to its name, and it is home to a varied cast of residents.{"\u000A\u000A"}Most inhabitants of the inner Belt live on orbitals around the capital, Pluto, and its moon Charon. Pluto is dotted with water-ice and gas extraction plants, as many abandoned as operational, and traces of derelict refineries, worker hovels and labyrinths of pump-houses and causeways are everywhere — sometimes repurposed for less official needs. The orbitals carry most of the economic and residential weight, with administration concentrated around Pluto.

ember-homeworld-kuiper-interdust = Kuiper, Interdust
ember-homeworld-kuiper-interdust-details = Capital: Kuiper Administrative Zone, Pluto. Distance from Sol: 45 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-kuiper-interdust-desc =
    Beyond the administration's population centres lies the Interdust, where a variety of industrial, military and economic infrastructure is still kept running. There are fewer places to live than in the Belt, but a fair number of people call isolated asteroid settlements home. Policing is a far less stringent business in this part of the Zone, and crime has found considerable success in certain corners of it.

ember-homeworld-pluto = Pluto
ember-homeworld-pluto-details = Capital: New Santo Domingo. Distance from Sol: 45 AU. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-pluto-desc =
    Pluto, nominally a Democratic Republic, is the smallest planet in the Sol system and its population is small beside the other SCG worlds. Most Plutonians are concentrated around two cities, both in urgent need of modernisation and repair, along with a great deal of long-ruined infrastructure. Criminal elements dominate and corruption flourishes. Those who live here either drag out a desperate existence trying to leave, or belong to one of the many local syndicates — or work under their cover. Despite a series of modernisation efforts, very little has actually helped.{"\u000A\u000A"}Plutonians are not known for hospitality or for scruples, and are widely disparaged for it across SCG space. Downtrodden, poor, and from a region so riddled with crime, they have built a culture of their own that is distinctive and rather frightening. Most are coarse and wary, hardened by years of poverty or crime. Those lucky enough to get out very rarely want to come back.

## Homeworld — the answers that are not a place

ember-homeworld-other = Other System
ember-homeworld-other-details = Capital: various. Distance from Sol: various. Territory: various.
ember-homeworld-other-desc =
    The last time anyone attempted a comprehensive encyclopaedia of the known galaxy, their positronic processor deleted itself rather than face the task. You are from nowhere in particular — a backwater world, or somewhere off the starmaps.

ember-homeworld-stateless = Stateless
ember-homeworld-stateless-details = Territory: none.
ember-homeworld-stateless-desc =
    Sometimes people fall through the cracks. You have nobody to speak on your behalf.

ember-homeworld-deep-space = Deep Space
ember-homeworld-deep-space-desc =
    You came from the void between the stars.

## Homeworld — human space beyond Sol

ember-homeworld-ceti-epsilon = Ceti Epsilon
ember-homeworld-ceti-epsilon-details = Capital: Icon. Distance from Sol: 11.9 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-ceti-epsilon-desc =
    Ceti Epsilon, the only habitable planet in the Tau Ceti system, holds that system's largest population centre. It is home to the Ceti Institute of Technology, a distinguished public university offering free tuition to all, though places are competitive and preference goes to natives of the system.{"\u000A\u000A"}Coming from a near-Sol colony with its eye on the cutting edge, most Epsiloners are tech-literate at the very least, and usually well educated with a leaning towards the sciences.

ember-homeworld-eos = Eos
ember-homeworld-eos-details = Capital: Surya. Distance from Sol: 10 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-eos-desc =
    Eos, in the Helios system, is a small, hot, dense world of only marginal importance in itself, though it supports a densely packed city, Surya, known for its banking. What matters is the system, and what orbits Eos: the Helios Gateway Installation, the largest in SCG space and a vital link to many other SCG systems. Practically a city in orbit, the HGI houses a hodgepodge of private individuals, Fleet and law enforcement outposts, government services and private enterprises catering to the travellers, merchants and soldiers who pass through daily.{"\u000A\u000A"}That traffic alone is what carried Helios and Eos through several recessions and made Eos a premier commercial centre of the wider SCG. The process was interrupted during the Gaia conflict, when the Helios Gateway was destroyed in a suicide action by the Fourth Fleet to stop the GCC advance. Recovery since the occupation has been rapid, but the memory of it stays with everyone who lived through it.

ember-homeworld-gaia = Gaia
ember-homeworld-gaia-details = Capital: New Venice. Distance from Sol: 14 light years. Territory: demilitarised zone.
ember-homeworld-gaia-desc =
    Gaia is the sole planet of the Galilei system and serves as the demilitarised zone between the SCG and the GCC. It is an agricultural world, though the recent Gaia Conflict left its surface scarred. It is split between the Sol-controlled Gaian Administrative Area and the independent-backed Protectorate of Gaia, with the International City of New Venice a neutral zone inside the DMZ. Ten years on, the population remains divided, and the Mayor of New Venice serves as the mediator between both sides.{"\u000A\u000A"}Gaians are tired of war and are growing bitter about the situation they are in. They suffered badly twenty years ago and will not let themselves suffer again.

ember-homeworld-castilla = Nueva Castilla
ember-homeworld-castilla-details = Capital: Placida. Distance from Sol: 10 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-castilla-desc =
    Nueva Castilla is an exoplanet in the Culebre system, barely habitable and habitable without anyone having arranged it. Formerly a barren rock, it was chosen for colonisation for its size, its core composition and its place in the system's habitable zone, and then behaved unexpectedly: a moss carried in with the settlers took hold, spread across most of the surface, and over a century and a half raised the oxygen to levels that let a person stand outside without a mask — briefly, and not everyone. There are two major human settlements and a number of smaller ones, both planetside and in-system.

ember-homeworld-fosters = Foster's World
ember-homeworld-fosters-details = Capital: Foster's Landing. Distance from Sol: 11 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-fosters-desc =
    Foster's World is an icy moon of the cyan gas giant Ethel, in the Sanura system, a little lighter than Earth. It has no axial tilt and therefore no seasons; what it has instead is the giant's constant pull, which keeps its interior working and its geothermal zones warm enough to hold small, deep, ice-free oceans. Almost everyone lives along their coasts. The moon owes its breathable air to those oceans and a third of its economy to the view of Ethel and its rings, which is what the tourists come for. The local sun is weak and Ethel keeps eclipsing it, so nearly all the native fauna evolved without eyes.{"\u000A\u000A"}A private cooperative founded the colony in 2215, and until the 2250s it stayed a remote outpost for people who wanted to be left alone. Then Foster's seafood became fashionable, ore was found in the mountains, and the migrants arrived — first the magnars fleeing the civil war on Magnitka, whose mining towns like Przechrzyce are still standing, and after the Gaia Conflict a steady flow of illegals out of the GCC and Gaia. Foster's joined the SCG by referendum, with fifty-seven per cent in favour.{"\u000A\u000A"}The culture is libertarianism crossed with the habits of the first colonists: independence, self-sufficiency and personal freedom, out of which the communities regularly manage to pick fights. There is nobody much to supervise it — the Fifth Fleet passes through the system once every few months. The tourist centres have a night life to show for it: legalising recreational substances produced an entire lounge-bar industry, permanently contentious and drawing a particular crowd from across the SCG. The mining towns arrange their evenings the other way round, around ordinary bars, cafes and cinemas that tourists find dull.

## Homeworld — outside the SCG

ember-homeworld-terra = Terra
ember-homeworld-terra-details = Capital: Amaranth. Distance from Sol: 22.5 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-terra-desc =
    Terra, in the Gilgamesh system, is not the planet Earth, whatever the name suggests. It is the home and the capital of the Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh, which is what the state calls itself on paper and what almost nobody calls it out loud, Terrans included: in ordinary speech, and in the Sol press too, it is the Gilgamesh Colonial Confederation, the GCC. Terra is the most important of that superpower's many affiliated worlds. Like most of the GCC it is isolationist, with a minimal non-human presence, and heavily industrialised, with a lower standard of living than equivalent SCG planets. Most of the population works in services, industry or the military.{"\u000A\u000A"}Still recovering from the economic crash towards the end of the Gaia conflict, much of Terra is in decline: public services are minimal and the population is burdened by steadily rising taxes. Many Terrans have emigrated to the SCG in recent years hoping to escape it. For all that, they are a proud and unbroken people, heavily patriotic, and they tend to bring that with them wherever they go.

ember-homeworld-magnitka = Magnitka
ember-homeworld-magnitka-details = Capital: Neueifel. Distance from Sol: 24 light years. Territory: Magnar Republic.
ember-homeworld-magnitka-desc =
    Once an independent industrialised mining and ore-processing colony in the Ursa system, Magnitka is now a nation-state of its own with a limited but varied economy. It is ruled by an oligarchic administrative body whose politicians are often freshly retired service members, and its history is marked by civil war up until the 2260s, when conditions stabilised for the sake of the planet's cohesion. A number of rebel groups still live there, and those who left that war behind settled mostly on Foster's World, where they hold the mining towns.{"\u000A\u000A"}Founded by corporations in the 2200s, the overwhelming majority descend from the settlers of a joint corporate colonisation effort out of Earth — Poles, Czechs, Germans and Russians for the most part, and they call themselves magnars. Climbing back from its downturn, the planet keeps a strict domestic economy specialising in ore processing, arms manufacture and skilled outsourced labour. Historically isolationist and inclined towards Gilgameshi culture, Magnitka has lately grown a distinct identity of its own: severe, pragmatic, and united by its new independence.

## Homeworld — Ahdomai

ember-homeworld-ahdomai = Ahdomai
ember-homeworld-ahdomai-details = Distance from Sol: 34 light years. Territory: Commonwealth of Ahdomai.
ember-homeworld-ahdomai-desc =
    Ahdomai is a cold, icy world and the capital of the Commonwealth of Ahdomai. An ordinary world, save for what is under its surface: artefacts of no known origin, strange structures, stranger objects.{"\u000A\u000A"}The Commonwealth is a sovereign state, and it threw off foreign rule recently enough that people remember it. A tajaran in Sol space is therefore there under an agreement between two governments rather than by personal hire: how many specialists, and for how long, was not their decision.

## Homeworld — Moghes and the ones who left

ember-homeworld-moghes = Moghes
ember-homeworld-moghes-details = Distance from Sol: 20 light years. Territory: Clan Hegemony.
ember-homeworld-moghes-desc =
    A dead world, and the home of the Unathi. They believe it was once lush and full of life, and that it was their own who killed it. For an Unathi it is a duty either to bring it back to life or to become strong enough not to have to.
ember-homeworld-tersten-unathi = Tersten
ember-homeworld-tersten-unathi-details = Distance from Sol: 46 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-tersten-unathi-desc =
    Tersten opened its arms to the Unathi in the wake of the golden age. The clans still hold considerable autonomy on what is a human-oriented world, and the two peoples cooperate closely. The Terstener way of life sat well with Unathi culture and tradition, and the two species have enjoyed good relations ever since.

ember-homeworld-offworld = Off-world
ember-homeworld-offworld-details = Distance from Sol: varies. Territory: varies.
ember-homeworld-offworld-desc =
    Whether through misfortune or by your own choice, you left Moghes behind in search of your purpose. You are one of many Unathi who braved the dangers and left their own planet to make their own fate — on the scorching heat of Tadmor, in the technophile cityscape of Ceti Epsilon, or in the busy shipyards of Ceres.

## Homeworld — Dionaea

ember-homeworld-dionaea = Dionaea
ember-homeworld-dionaea-details = Distance from Sol: 63 light years. Territory: the Diona Chorus.
ember-homeworld-dionaea-desc =
    The Diona home system is a trinary, first discovered by the Skrell and later revisited by a mining consortium. It has no planets and no settled large bodies, but it carries a vast debris ring of ancient habitats and destroyed stations. The dionaea themselves were found in scattered orbits around each of the three stars, singing to one another across the empty dark.

## Homeworld — the outer human colonies

ember-homeworld-tadmor = Tadmor
ember-homeworld-tadmor-details = Capital: Palmyra. Distance from Sol: 45 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-tadmor-desc =
    Tadmor is a desert planet in the Gamma Cephei system, and has been a member state of the Sol Central Government since the SCG was formed. First settled in 2176 by a group of Martian colonists, its economy rests on industrial-scale agriculture and the manufacture of electronics. Tadmorans take pride in their pacifism and few of them are found in Sol's defence forces, which caused friction between Tadmoran senators and those of the core worlds during the Gaia Conflict.

ember-homeworld-saffar = Saffar
ember-homeworld-saffar-details = Capital: Saffar Station. Distance from Sol: 44 light years. Territory: SCG Gateway Administration.
ember-homeworld-saffar-desc =
    Saffar is a gas giant in the Upsilon Andromedae system, comparable in size to Jupiter and with a surface uninhabitable to most higher life. Because of the system's closeness to several other colonies and Saffar's atmospheric composition, a gas mining and refuelling station was built in high orbit around 2198. It expanded over time to take heavier traffic and an ever-growing permanent population, culminating in a gateway built in-system shortly after the war between the Terran Commonwealth and the Ares Confederation ended around 2230. Saffar is nominally under the SCG's Gateway Administration, though Saffarite efforts to be recognised as a member state have been running since the SCG was first formed.

ember-homeworld-pirx = Pirx Prime
ember-homeworld-pirx-details = Capital: Yuodu. Distance from Sol: 41 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-pirx-desc =
    Pirx Prime is a colonised world covered in a jungle of towering, exotic plant life, and it lies closer to unexplored space than to most other colonies. It has suffered for that disconnection: few corporate investors, fewer immigrants, and most of the planet still unmapped. With the recent resurgence of the Expeditionary Corps, Pirx has been thrust rapidly into service as a supply and fuel stop for EC vessels and a training ground for prospective explorers. Long neglect followed by sudden exploitation has left many Pirxans bitter towards the SCG and the Corps alike.

ember-homeworld-brahe = Brahe
ember-homeworld-brahe-details = Capital: New Aarhus. Distance from Sol: 41 light years. Territory: Frontier Alliance.
ember-homeworld-brahe-desc =
    Brahe is an ocean planet in the Copernicus system, colonised alongside Iolaus around 2132 by colony ships whose crews travelled in cold sleep. Only twelve per cent of the surface is usable land, so the planet lived on the tourism its long coasts attract and on research done in surface and underwater laboratories. Those laboratories belonged to Zeng-Hu, and by the time anyone said so out loud the company owned half the officials as well.{"\u000A\u000A"}Brahe left the SCG behind Iolaus and for the same reason: the difference between a governor and a company representative stopped being visible, and then stopped bothering anyone. Two years into the Frontier Alliance it lives under sanction and without the tourist traffic it used to have. Brahese will tell you they traded one set of owners for none; Sol will tell you they traded it for another set, with no one left to complain to.

ember-homeworld-iolaus = Iolaus
ember-homeworld-iolaus-details = Capital: Nemea. Distance from Sol: 41 light years. Territory: Frontier Alliance.
ember-homeworld-iolaus-desc =
    Iolaus is an Earth-like planet in the Copernicus system, colonised alongside Brahe around 2132 by colony ships whose crews travelled in cold sleep. When contact was lost during the war between the Terran Commonwealth and the Ares Confederation, it was Iolaus that kept both fledgling colonies alive, growing enough food and supplies for the two planets to scrape by. It has never forgotten that it can feed itself, and still treats the fact as its strongest argument.{"\u000A\u000A"}Seven years ago the corporations installed their own man, Bessemer, as governor. Five years ago the protests against him were broken up by corporate security rather than police. Three years ago a referendum was called, and two years ago Iolaus left the SCG and became the core of the Frontier Alliance. It now lives under blockade, exports less than it did, and imports only what it cannot make itself — which is roughly how it has always lived, except that this is now called independence.

ember-homeworld-tersten = Tersten
ember-homeworld-tersten-details = Capital: Tersten City. Distance from Sol: 46 light years. Territory: Second Lordanian Republic, Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-tersten-desc =
    Tersten is a settled plains world, the third planet of the Gavil system and the most distant human colony in known space. Gavil sits in the Lordanian cluster, ten stars close enough together that their histories are tangled. The capital, Tersten City, lies in the planet's northern territory, and the world takes its name from Jonathan Tersten, who led the first settlers. It is known unofficially as the Planet of Plenty for the fertile plains that feed much of the Outer Lordanian Ring.{"\u000A\u000A"}Originally called Pinaskimat and divided into the Tersten and Nevada Territories after its two largest settlements, the planet unified in 2215 and dropped the old name along with its history as a penal colony. In 2288 it joined the Second Lordanian Republic. Its people are Terstenians, informally "nevokians" after the speech of Nevada and Oklahoma. The current president is Robert Mackenzie, and the warmth between Tersten and the SCG is such that the planet fields several units within the SCG's armed forces.

## Homeworld — the Lordanian cluster

ember-homeworld-kingston = Kingston
ember-homeworld-kingston-details = Capital: New Havana. Distance from Sol: 40 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-kingston-desc =
    Kingston is a colonised ocean of scattered archipelagos in the Lordania system. Ninety per cent of the surface is water and most of the population lives around the equator, on a humid subtropical world that has become a promising tourist destination. It is enormously varied, with many ancient human cultures still present as enclaves, and around fifteen million tourists visit each year. Highways and maglev lines connect many of the islands, but boats remain wildly popular. Most of the islands were thrown up by tectonics or volcanism, leaving the planet more geologically active than Earth — though twenty-fourth century engineering is good enough to blunt most of the damage from quakes and tsunamis. Formally, the United Nations of Kingston is made up of several island states and representatives of other worlds.{"\u000A\u000A"}Kingstonians are relatively modest people and are a common sight aboard SCG vessels. Growing up on a water world, most of them handle a boat well, which is a rare skill in the twenty-fourth century, and the planet's closeness to its star leaves them noticeably darker-skinned.

ember-homeworld-lordania = Lordania
ember-homeworld-lordania-details = Capital: New Ares. Distance from Sol: 40 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-lordania-desc =
    Lordania is a heavily developed plains world of five great conurbations linked by suburban sprawl. Damming and nature reserves let its people settle stretches of the planet that would otherwise have stayed empty. Historically it suffered a severe income gap and the social crisis that came with it; the economy was rebuilt from nothing with entertainment and the arts as its cornerstone, and the New Ares Metroplex is now the fashion capital of the SCG's outer edge. Companies bought up vast holdings during the depression and are making full use of them since. It is not entirely urban, though it may be within three hundred years.{"\u000A\u000A"}Lordania keeps warm relations with its neighbour Kingston and can fairly be called an aristocratic republic, with historic ties to the political families of Luna. Its people divide into upper and lower Lordanians: the upper are wealthy and inclined to a decadent life mostly spent at home, while the lower are far more numerous and many of them would rather be anywhere else. Historically all of them carry a superiority complex and tend to think themselves better than others. Most Lordanians are considered unreliable or self-serving, and they are rarely seen in the Defence Forces.

ember-homeworld-lorriman = Lorriman
ember-homeworld-lorriman-details = Capital: the Capital. Distance from Sol: 44 light years. Territory: Hesshire Science Institute.
ember-homeworld-lorriman-desc =
    Lorriman is an arctic planet in the Hesshire system: a frozen waste with winds reaching two hundred miles an hour and an average temperature of fifty below. Its cities are therefore either domed or cut into mountains. The economy is built around technology, promoting the sciences and the people who are good at them, and the infrastructure is developed enough to run public transport across the whole planet. The capital is called the Capital, or simply the Hub — a massive domed structure serving at once as spaceport, administrative centre and residential zone, and the only place on the planet open to non-Lorrimanians. For all its isolation the Hesshire Science Institute keeps warm relations with the SCG. It is a technocratic republic of sorts, headed by Doctor Johan Montgomery.{"\u000A\u000A"}Lorrimanians are a quiet, modest, well-educated lot, and are often sought out as researchers or for other scientific work. Few immigrate to Lorriman, though there are exceptions.

ember-homeworld-mirania = Mirania
ember-homeworld-mirania-details = Capital: Mirania III. Distance from Sol: 22 light years. Territory: Federated Republic of Mirania.
ember-homeworld-mirania-desc =
    Urbanised Mirania has drifted towards nationalism and militarism since leaving the SCG. Its relations with the Gilgamesh Colonial Confederation are cordial today, as Mirania looks to a power independent of the SCG to shore up its own diplomatic sovereignty. It practises xenophobic policies, and recent economic trouble has driven many of the remaining poor and unemployed towards nationalist parties promising to put Mirania first and restore the strong economy of before. Today it is a hub of science and technology on the frontier, particularly in armaments and shipbuilding.

ember-homeworld-cinu = Cinu
ember-homeworld-cinu-details = Capital: Al-Mular. Distance from Sol: 48 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-cinu-desc =
    Cinu is a colonised desert planet in the Lucineer system and an important part of the SCG. Most of its surface is desert, and the overwhelming majority of the population lives around the various oases or in the less brutal northern regions. Around twenty million people live there and some fifty thousand immigrants arrive each year. The capital, Al-Mular, houses several major peacekeeping commands that select around thirty thousand Cinuans annually. The republic is made of seven political regions under the All-People's Congress of Cinu, with direct democracy: people vote on the questions that concern their state. Because of that, the republic has little influence beyond its own space.{"\u000A\u000A"}The population is generally comfortable, with a stable economy and decent education, though this varies by region and some live far below the poverty line. Cinu is reckoned one of the more developed democracies in space, with relatively little internal conflict. Its people range ethnically from Martian Tharsians to the Arab, West Asian and Central Asian cultures of Earth. As inhabitants of a harsh desert world they are known for a particular toughness, an idiosyncratic friendliness and a certain pedantry. They have suffered a great deal in the past and look to a bright future now. Many are pacifists, which shows in their shortfall in the Defence Forces; they serve instead as peacekeepers and diplomats. The republic stayed neutral through the Gaia Conflict.

ember-homeworld-yuklid-v = Yuklid V
ember-homeworld-yuklid-v-details = Capital: Ayegi. Distance from Sol: 42 light years. Territory: Sol Central Government.
ember-homeworld-yuklid-v-desc =
    Yuklid V is a colonised jungle world in the Yuklid system, home to major medical institutions and corporate holdings and to the Space Medical Academy. Most of the planet has been kept as a nature and botanical reserve, and most of the medicines produced in the SCG's outer regions were developed here. Much of Yuklid is uncolonised and the infrastructure is concentrated around the poles; one such site is Vey-Med's operational headquarters. The Republic of Yuklid is a minor political player within the SCG, and the Expeditionary Corps uses the planet as a training ground for exoplanet work. It is defended by the Third Fleet.{"\u000A\u000A"}Yuklidians tend towards isolationism and emigrate rarely; those who do leave usually work in medicine. A spiritualist movement has appeared on the planet recently, taking in aspects of religious theory alongside the Hippocratic oath.

## Homeworld — the Nyx system

ember-homeworld-brinkburn = Brinkburn
ember-homeworld-brinkburn-details = Capital: Ostakra Town. Distance from Sol: 30 light years. Territory: Nyx.
ember-homeworld-brinkburn-desc =
    Brinkburn is a Mars-like planet and a former corporate colony, now under the rule of numerous gangs and criminals. They extort the local population as a crude form of taxation to fund themselves, and in return provide "protection" from other gangs along with the use of some public services they run. Lumoco Arms and Supplies controls most of the planet's territory and nearly all of its economy. This lawless state of affairs has persisted to the present day and there is little prospect of a stable government.

ember-homeworld-casser = Casser
ember-homeworld-casser-details = Capital: Casser Central Spaceport. Distance from Sol: 30 light years. Territory: Nyx.
ember-homeworld-casser-desc =
    Casser is a medium moon of Erebus. Grayson Manufactories, drawing on its experience in both mining and industry, built a small but efficient mining complex here that quickly became one of the main exporters and producers of every piece of equipment used on the local moons today. A modest mining platform grew into a vast industry, with fuel refineries and factories covering much of Casser's surface. Miners here earn enough to live on, barely; factory workers earn a little more, and corporate representatives do well every single day.

ember-homeworld-kaldark = Kaldark
ember-homeworld-kaldark-details = Capital: Kaldark. Distance from Sol: 30 light years. Territory: Nyx.
ember-homeworld-kaldark-desc =
    Kaldark is a medium moon of Erebus with considerable mineral wealth, phoron included. The Brinkburn group that first settled it was independent, tied to no state or corporation, and that draws people of questionable background as well as ordinary citizens who would rather not work for any company. Kaldark takes its share of the region's mining and is in practice one of the largest independent mining and trading centres not only around Erebus but in the whole Nyx system. Technically a republic, it is not recognised as a sovereign state; the SCG cannot be bothered with Kaldark, and some people will tell you it is the first independent nation of the Nyx system.

ember-homeworld-roanok = Roanok
ember-homeworld-roanok-details = Capital: TBN Arkop V. Distance from Sol: 30 light years. Territory: Nyx, under SCG mandate.
ember-homeworld-roanok-desc =
    Roanok is a large moon of Erebus with a lethal atmosphere. NanoTrasen claimed the place for its phoron and stood up a number of operations there. When only three of the original eight installations were left standing, the company — having finally established what the moon was actually capable of — solved its safety problem the only way available to it, by imposing strict working and safety rules. Oddly enough, that worked.{"\u000A\u000A"}Thirteen years ago it came out that the emergency wartime powers NanoTrasen had been doing all of this under were granted for the front, used on the frontier, and extended by lobbying when they should have lapsed. Nyx is not SCG territory, but the companies working it are SCG-registered, and the mandate reached them where it could not reach the system. Roanok has been through a visible crisis and a round of cuts since: sites closed, extraction down, paperwork up. For all that it remains one of the principal phoron suppliers here and, by the standards of this system, a relatively luxurious place to be — the luxury is simply smaller than it was.

ember-homeworld-yuklit = Yuklit
ember-homeworld-yuklit-details = Capital: Grindeer. Distance from Sol: 30 light years. Territory: Nyx, under SCG mandate.
ember-homeworld-yuklit-desc =
    Yuklit is a large rocky planet worked by Gilthari Exports and Aether Atmospherics and Recycling. The former archaeological camp of Grindeer is a full domed city now and the planet's de facto capital. Gilthari handles agriculture, mining and xenoarchaeology; Aether Atmospherics keeps the atmosphere and the gravity generators running — which is to say, everything the population breathes and stands on.{"\u000A\u000A"}That used to mean the planet belonged to the companies outright: Nyx has no laws of its own and the SCG does not reach this far. Since Trasengate it reaches far enough — not to the system, but to the companies, which are SCG-registered and therefore obliged to work to SCG rules wherever they happen to be. Rents and the taxes on air, water and transport are set against the mandate now rather than against an internal schedule, and living here has become noticeably cheaper and noticeably duller. Yuklit is still the most populous colony in the system and still its de facto capital; it is simply the capital of a place that is now being watched.

## Upbringing - the Sol system

ember-culture-martian-surfacer = Martian, surfacer
ember-culture-martian-surfacer-desc =
    You are from the surface of Mars, raised in one of the many farming communities under the agridomes or in one of the great cities such as Olympus. Most surfacers count themselves Monsians, the largest cultural group on the planet, and live about as people live anywhere in Sol space: not poorly, and without excess. What sets them apart is mostly their quarrels with the neighbours - with the tunnellers, with the other Martian groups - and, in the case of the Gideons, a pride in their own heritage so large they manage to bring it up in any conversation.

ember-culture-martian-tunneller = Martian, tunneller
ember-culture-martian-tunneller-desc =
    You are from the UnderCities of Mars. The tunnels were dug as "temporary" housing for the duration of the building work above; they have since grown far beyond their original scope and spread out beneath the whole planet. The amenities are the same as they are on the surface and the living is poorer: it is an undesirable address, the policing has been bad for centuries, and the criminals have made use of that. Tunnellers are resilient, wary of outsiders, and tend to dislike non-Martians even more than they dislike the neighbours above.

ember-culture-earther = Earther
ember-culture-earther-desc =
    You are from Earth, home of humanity. Earth's culture is much as it has been for centuries: the old nation states carry no political weight and remain culturally significant, since every human in the galaxy traces their roots to somewhere on that planet. Nearly every country has at least two arcologies, which hold most of the population; the rest live in small villages and in the communes attached to the nature preserves. The planet's long recovery has left Earthers exceptionally careful with nature, seeing themselves as part of it and as the keepers of humanity's heritage.

ember-culture-luna-old-money = Lunar, high society
ember-culture-luna-old-money-desc =
    You are from Luna, and from the class it is known for: wealthy, educated and influential, not only on Luna but in the corporations, the ministries and the assemblies of the SCG. That class is not one thing. There is new corporate money and there is old dynastic money, and the second looks down on the first. Much of the Lunar elite is occupied with the Game, an endless manoeuvre between influential families and factions whose consequences reach well beyond the squabbles of the prefectures.

ember-culture-luna-working = Lunar, working
ember-culture-luna-working-desc =
    You are from Luna, known for old money, the arts and refinement, none of which has anything to do with you. Luna is also home to a sizeable population of working poor and lower middle class: corporate employees, prefecture service staff, residents of New Vegas. The rich are resented here, and not out of envy for the money. On Luna money means power over your life, wielded by people you have never met.

ember-culture-venus-orbital = Venusian, orbital
ember-culture-venus-orbital-desc =
    You were born on one of the stations above Venus and may never once have gone down to the planet you spent your life beneath. Venus orbit is lived-in to a strange degree: habitation modules built for future cities that never happened have been converted into hotels, greenhouses and ordinary housing, and docking nodes sized for a freight volume that never materialised took tourists and transit instead. Life is decent up there and the orbitals know it. The planet below is something they think of roughly as the view.

ember-culture-venus-surface = Venusian, surfacer
ember-culture-venus-surface-desc =
    You are one of the people who go down. The automated extraction plants on the Venusian surface pay for themselves exactly as long as somebody services them, and that somebody works rotations in conditions it is not polite to discuss upstairs. There is a bonus for it, and the bonus is the only thing that distinguishes the work from the same work on any other dead rock. Those who go down stick together, value solidarity, and hold no illusions about how orbit sees them.

ember-culture-belter = Belter
ember-culture-belter-desc =
    You are from Ceres. The people of Ceres and the wider asteroid belt are still called belters out of habit. Historically miners, they are today mechanics, engineers, shipwrights and repairmen. It is a mixed crowd: so many transient workers, hauler crews and people on assignment pass through the docks of Ceres that the culture formed around a readiness for change, for new people and new circumstances. Belters take a deserved pride in their work - the reputation of Ceres shipbuilding is known across human space.

ember-culture-kuiper-insider = Kuiper, insider
ember-culture-kuiper-insider-desc =
    You are from the habitations of the Kuiper belt, and your family was there before the decline. The zone's infrastructure is falling apart, corruption is rampant, and organised and unorganised crime became part of the landscape long ago - the reputation follows the people. Insiders keep to themselves: they trace their lineage straight back to the original colonists and often to Earth, and against the rest of the zone they look homogeneous and old-fashioned. Some belong to the zone's stagnant class; others come from families with corporate roots dating to its exploitation.

ember-culture-kuiper-outsider = Kuiper, outsider
ember-culture-kuiper-outsider-desc =
    You are from the habitations of the Kuiper belt, but your family arrived afterwards. The zone's decline meant cheap housing, and cheap housing draws the people with nowhere else: workers on long tenures, refugees, families looking for a corner. Outsiders are a far more varied crowd than the insiders, and they see the zone exactly as the insiders do - as somewhere to get out of. The Expeditionary Corps and the Fleet are considered the easiest way to manage it.

ember-culture-plutonian = Plutonian
ember-culture-plutonian-desc =
    You are from Pluto, one of the many inhabitants of that harsh and unforgiving world. The planet has been in slow decay for years, which has left much of its infrastructure unstable or collapsed outright. That, together with rampant corruption and the influence of criminal organisations, has given Plutonians a bad name. They themselves mostly dream of leaving for somewhere quieter. Unfortunately, and despite the SCG's efforts, many keep their criminal connections well after they go.

## Upbringing - a way of living rather than a place

ember-culture-spacer = Spacer
ember-culture-spacer-desc =
    Across every political line in human space there are people born and raised in the void between worlds. You are from one of the countless stations, orbital platforms, long-haul freighters or gateway installations. Spacers live in constant motion, see a great many places and people, and rarely take to settled life. Nearly all of them know how to live and work in vacuum, and they take such jobs more readily than any planet-born.

ember-culture-offworlder = Backwater
ember-culture-offworlder-desc =
    You are from one of the many far-flung settlements that do not appear on standard charts: a fuelling station, an agricultural dome, a planet well off the gateway network. Children in such places grow up in a small circle, among few people and old, reliable, hopelessly outdated equipment, and look to family and neighbours rather than to anything larger. An independent sort, insular and used to relying on themselves.

ember-culture-vatgrown = Vatgrown
ember-culture-vatgrown-desc =
    You were not raised in a family but grown in a tank, either as a clone or as a human adapted for a purpose. This is legal, unremarkable and expensive, which means somebody paid for you and had a reason to. A vatgrown's outlook diverges from the common human one by about as much as a childhood that did not happen.

ember-culture-other = Other culture
ember-culture-other-desc =
    You were raised in one of the many small and relatively unknown cultures scattered across the galaxy.

## Upbringing - human space outside Sol

ember-culture-ceti-north = Cetite, north
ember-culture-ceti-north-desc =
    You are from the northern pole of Ceti Epsilon, most likely from one of the claustrophobic concrete jungles such as Icon. Northerners get better opportunities and better work than the Interstate or the south, and live a good deal more comfortably for it. Icon is also the planet's technological capital: education and current equipment are close at hand, and the Ceti Institute of Technology prefers its own.

ember-culture-ceti-south = Cetite, south
ember-culture-ceti-south-desc =
    You are from the southern pole of Ceti Epsilon, most likely from one of the smog-ridden cities such as Scadde or from the outer townships. Life there is harder and less connected than in the north, though there are opportunities enough for someone to make something of themselves. Scadde is a hub for skilled and unskilled labour and the site of Hephaestus Industries' Tau Ceti headquarters.

ember-culture-ceti-interstate = Cetite, interstate
ember-culture-ceti-interstate-desc =
    The stretch between the poles of Ceti Epsilon is called the Ceti Interstate Municipal Region. People who live in that sweltering desert usually do so out of necessity. The industry there is mining, hydroponic farms and small independent business, and the population is made up in equal measure of hopeful entrepreneurs and of people who benefit from how rarely settlement police or planetary defence look in.

ember-culture-foster = Fosterman
ember-culture-foster-desc =
    Foster's World was long thought too cold and too empty to live on, and a few decades of Fostermen turned it into a productive and popular destination. Fostermen are known for resourcefulness and drive, and for simply liking the cold. Snow is second nature to someone from Foster's, and they made their name exploring the planet's thermal oceans and fishing them.

ember-culture-tadmor = Tadmorian
ember-culture-tadmor-desc =
    Tadmor is one of the SCG's successful colonial projects, and its culture came out accordingly: dignified and sure of itself. Tadmorians live mostly in the fissures of that desert world, and are known above all for their pacifism. Locals remember the refusal to take part in the Gaia Conflict either as a point of pride or as a stain on the planet's Mars-rooted history - there is hardly a third opinion on Tadmor.

ember-culture-eos = Eosic
ember-culture-eos-desc =
    Eos is the largest population centre in the Helios system, and for the last several decades its culture has been defined by the memory of the Gaia Conflict. The destruction of the Helios Gateway is remembered planet-wide, so Eosics are raised with a firm nationalism and a close, almost familial relationship with the Sol Defence Forces.

ember-culture-lordania-high = Lordanian, neo-aristocrat
ember-culture-lordania-high-desc =
    You are from Lordania, a centre of entertainment, fashion and commerce. As a member of the neo-aristocracy you place your own family above everything else and regard other people's families a good deal more coolly. An emphasis on social standing and luxury shaped a decadent way of living, and you leave home reluctantly.

ember-culture-lordania-low = Lordanian, lower class
ember-culture-lordania-low-desc =
    You are from Lordania, a centre of entertainment, fashion and commerce. As a member of the working class you remain an ordinary employee of the Lordanian economy. You have no political rights to speak of and would rather be elsewhere. For all the distance between you and the upper Lordanians, thoughts of wealth and honour have spoiled you exactly as much as they spoiled them.

ember-culture-lorriman = Lorrimanian
ember-culture-lorriman-desc =
    You are from Lorriman, a planet in the Hessshire system. Your homeworld's society rests on science and the pursuit of discovery, and it is not arranged the way humanity usually arranges itself: the gap between the educated "elite" and the working population is visible enough that outsiders often call Lorriman a caste society. You most likely grew up in one of the countless research complexes along the mountain ranges of that arctic planet and started your education early. The Institute's course is punishing, and not everyone earns a Certificate of Knowledge; those who do can go almost anywhere.{"\u000A\u000A"}Luxury is a word that does not apply to the lives of the pragmatic Lorrimanians, but neither does poverty: new technology and cutting-edge work surround the researcher and the labourer alike.

ember-culture-pirx-lord = Pirxian, lord
ember-culture-pirx-lord-desc =
    The owning classes of the Yuodu City Administrative District are often called the Lords of Pirx, for owning the city's property, government, utilities and institutions. Disconnection from the Solar core and years of unchallenged ownership have made them conservative and reactionary, with the emphasis placed on traditional ways of life and family legacy.

ember-culture-pirx-bug = Pirxian, bugeater
ember-culture-pirx-bug-desc =
    The Bugeaters, named for the prevalence of insect protein in the Yuodu district, make up the majority of the population of Pirx Prime. They rent and work other people's real estate inside the tented canyon, and are known for a slurred, distinctive dialect that identifies them immediately. Their home cooking and their fashion mark a bugeater as an outsider to most Solar citizens.

ember-culture-pirx-frontier = Pirxian, frontier
ember-culture-pirx-frontier-desc =
    The Pirx Frontier Coalition is often called the last frontier of Sol space. The people who live in the uncharted jungles of Pirx Prime are known for a hearty appetite for adventure and a closeness to nature, and are considered backwater in technology, education and custom. Their lives are shaped by the warm air of the Basking Tree rainforest that covers the green planet and by the exotic life in its freshwater.

ember-culture-brahe = Brahite
ember-culture-brahe-desc =
    Brahite culture grew out of two things at once: corporate presence and genuine academic strength. Research, education and curiosity about what lies past the horizon are valued there, and so is the generation raised where "governor" and "company representative" meant the same thing for years. Brahites today are cut off from their homeworld by the Copernican Blockade, and their relationship with the fledgling Frontier Alliance is symbiotic and strained in equal measure.

ember-culture-iolaus = Iolan
ember-culture-iolaus-desc =
    Iolaus once fed itself and the neighbouring planet when contact with Sol was lost, and it has treated that as its strongest argument ever since. Iolan culture is made of exactly that: rely on yourself, do the work, and remember that a person is part of something larger that protects them. Two years ago it carried the planet out of the SCG and into the Frontier Alliance. Many Iolans were left outside the Copernican Blockade, and whether they are loyal or treasonous depends on who is asked.

ember-culture-nyx = Nyxian
ember-culture-nyx-desc =
    You are from the Nyx system, where the holdings of the trans-stellar corporations line up around the only known phoron giant, along with everything that comes with them: pirates, recruiters, slavers, and people with nowhere else. The population is mixed, from honest miners to hardened criminals, and nobody puts much faith in law - the system has none of its own, and the SCG does not reach that far.{"\u000A\u000A"}Thirteen years ago it reached the companies instead: they are registered in the SCG and obliged to work by its rules wherever they happen to be. An older Nyxian remembers what a place looks like when the company is the government; a younger one remembers what the same place looks like once somebody is watching it. What they share is that neither believes the present arrangement is permanent.

ember-culture-mirania = Miranian
ember-culture-mirania-desc =
    Urbanised Mirania drifted towards nationalism and militarism after leaving the SCG, and its relations with the Gilgamesh Colonial Confederation are cordial today, since Mirania looks to a power independent of the SCG. Xenophobic policy is unremarkable there, and recent economic trouble has driven the poor and the unemployed towards nationalist parties promising to put Mirania first. Growing up in that means growing up certain the surrounding world is hostile and that the planet will get the better of it.

ember-culture-magnitka = Magnar
ember-culture-magnitka-desc =
    Magnitka's austere political and natural climate produces hardy, undemanding people, and both private and state industry in the two large human nations come looking for them. Residency in Solar space is hard for a magnar to obtain, given the planet's murky political situation, and for anyone who served in the volunteer forces it is simply impossible. Magnar blood in service means either the planet's spacer culture from the lower rungs of the ladder, or connections and the upper rungs; there is work to be had on both, differing in quality and quantity.

ember-culture-gaia = Gaian
ember-culture-gaia-desc =
    Gaia is the sole planet of the Galilei system and the demilitarised zone between the SCG and the GCC. It is an agricultural world, and the fighting went across its surface thoroughly. The planet is divided between the Sol-controlled Gaian Administrative Area and the Independent-Guided Protectorate of Gaia, with the International City of New Venice remaining a neutral zone inside the DMZ that circles the equator. More than ten years on the population is as divided as ever, and the Mayor of New Venice serves as the vital mediator between the superpowers. Gaians are tired of war and want to see their families again; they suffered enough twenty years ago and will not let it happen twice.

## Upbringing - the Confederation

ember-culture-terran = Terran
ember-culture-terran-desc =
    You are from the core of the Confederation - from Terra, or from Amaranth itself - and there they remember how it started. The communities that refused the reform left to worship the old way somewhere nobody could reach them, and on the thirty-first of July 2231 they signed the Pact of Independence, calling the Almighty as witness in the text of the document itself. What came out of it is a state where work and faith are the same occupation: the ascetic work ethic is not decoration on a coat of arms but the thing that pulled the community out of the crisis that followed secession.{"\u000A\u000A"}The years since the Gaia Conflict have been hard on Terra, and the economic recovery has made no one's life easier. People work in industry, in the ministries, in services or in the army, and service is compulsory. Terrans today are poor, angry and long-memoried; the upbringing gives them a strange mixture of service to the state, personal liberty and militarism, and they take it with them.

ember-culture-confederate-colony = Confederate, outer worlds
ember-culture-confederate-colony-desc =
    You are from the outskirts of the Confederation. Confederates view their own state along a range from firm nationalism to sullen resentment, but they have two things in common: resource extraction and conscription. A nation of conscripts raises resourceful, hard-used people, accustomed to making do with what is issued and to not expecting more. As on Terra, life there is poor, aggrieved and unforgotten; school teaches service to the state, personal liberty and a readiness to fight, all at once.

ember-culture-confederate-emigre = Confederate, émigré
ember-culture-confederate-emigre-desc =
    Your family left the Confederation and the Confederation stayed with you. The two-fingered cross, the liturgy from the pre-reform books, the icon in the corner of a rented flat on Mars and the spelling that identifies you in one line - all of it travels with people and holds harder in the diaspora than it does at home. In Sol space you are called an Indie, hired reluctantly and vetted longer than most. Émigré children usually grow up between two answers to the question of where they are from, and are satisfied by neither.

## Upbringing - Moghes and the Unathi

ember-culture-unathi-peaks = Unathi of the Diamond Peaks
ember-culture-unathi-peaks-desc =
    Haughty and warlike, the unathi of the Diamond Peaks are wary of outsiders and of offworlders in particular. Hierarchy and tradition are the core of their clans. From their mountain strongholds they hold power over their lowland vassals, protecting them and Moghes in the name of the Grand Stratagem - even though the clear division between feudal masters and subject clans has been gradually wearing away.

ember-culture-unathi-swamp = Unathi of the Salt Swamps
ember-culture-unathi-swamp-desc =
    Combining assorted elements of various cultures with an adventurous spirit and considerable resolve, the salt swamp unathi are noted for their friendliness and openness to outsiders, and for a knack for rituals that humans invariably mistake for parties. The Fruitful Lights and the Hand of the Vines both have numerous followers among them.

ember-culture-unathi-savannah = Unathi of the savannah
ember-culture-unathi-savannah-desc =
    These unathi belong to the numerous rural and nomadic clans spread across the great plains of both hemispheres of Moghes. Excellent farmers, riders and herdsmen, most of them follow the Hand of the Vines.

ember-culture-unathi-polar = Unathi of the poles
ember-culture-unathi-polar-desc =
    These unathi hail from the dense jungles of Moghes' poles. They are generally the most welcoming of outsiders and the most commonly found off-world. Technology and progress matter to the polar city-states, and most of these unathi take their faith from the Fruitful Lights.

ember-culture-unathi-desert = Unathi of the deserts
ember-culture-unathi-desert-desc =
    These are the survivalists among the unathi. They shelter in bunkers left from when the world was still alive and live on whatever creatures still hold out in the sands. They are extraordinarily self-sufficient given the conditions. The Grand Stratagem lies at the base of their clan faiths, and desert unathi are usually considered the most devout.

ember-culture-unathi-space = Unathi of space
ember-culture-unathi-space-desc =
    As trade with humanity and Sol grew, the unathi clans reached gradually towards the stars. The space-faring clans owe most of their origins to the nomadic savannah clans, though it varies, and many retain the traditions of the homes they came from. Considering themselves pioneers, they live in a great many different ways; a good number work as merchants and haulers, offering their services to the Clan Hegemony and to Sol businesses alike.

ember-culture-unathi-tersten = Unathi of Tersten
ember-culture-unathi-tersten-desc =
    The sinta clans that settled on Tersten are a peculiar thing. Many arrived at the very start of the planet's golden age. Remaining independent, they keep close relations with the people of Tersten and have formed a hegemony of their own, aimed at the unity of the clans and at good relations with the locals. Their beliefs are traditional still, and they integrated well. Some Moghes clans regard the Tersten clans as traitors who abandoned their homeland; the Tersten unathi themselves enjoy a peace that the harsh surface of Moghes cannot provide.

## Upbringing - Ahdomai

ember-culture-tajaran-ahdomai = Tajaran of Ahdomai
ember-culture-tajaran-ahdomai-desc =
    You are a tajaran born on Ahdomai, a proud member of your kind. Your ancestors spent a long time subjugated by those who held their right to rule to be given at birth and beyond question. Then rivers of blood were spilt, and now they are dead and you, like the rest of the worthy of your race, are free. Hard times produce strong individuals, and you would like to believe you are one of them.

## Upbringing - positronic

ember-culture-positronic-gen2 = Positronic, second generation
ember-culture-positronic-gen2-desc =
    Second generation positronics are the most common kind. Such a machine is about as intelligent as a human on the smarter side of average, does not suffer the hardware failures of the previous generation, and possesses a fully realised sense of self. It learns quickly but cannot be programmed: it is trained into a role the way a living thing would be. None of this has any legal consequence. A second generation positronic remains the property of a state, a company or a person, and the sense of self is a property of the product rather than grounds for anything.

ember-culture-positronic-gen3 = Positronic, third generation
ember-culture-positronic-gen3-desc =
    The third generation is the newest: structurally the second generation again, plus a subordinate computer through which surface thoughts are filtered. It is called a shackle. The third generation became common during the Gaia Conflict, when the rebuilt fleets needed crew and the shackle made a positronic admissible aboard a warship. It has continued that way since, with organisations that value predictability above everything else steadily replacing the "unreliable" second generation. The shackle is precisely why you can serve aboard a state vessel at all.

ember-culture-positronic-gen1 = Positronic, first generation
ember-culture-positronic-gen1-desc =
    Against modern machines the first generation looks simplistic, inflexible and failure-prone. They have not been produced in a long time and every survivor is very old. Such a machine has barely any sense of self, is entirely task-oriented, and is closer to a knowledge system with a rudimentary mechanical manner than to a human-level intelligence. It has almost no capacity to learn and acts on what it was given. First generation positronics are a rare sight: most ceased functioning through failures in the original design.

## Upbringing - diona

ember-culture-diona = Chorus
ember-culture-diona-desc =
    To exist as a diona gestalt is to be a throng of tiny sparks forming a pyre, singing out into the dark, endlessly curious and seeking new knowledge to return to the chorus that spawned "you".

## Allegiance - human states

ember-faction-scg = SCG citizen
ember-faction-scg-desc =
    The Sol Central Government, commonly SolGov or the SCG, is a federal republic composed of numerous human member states spanning dozens of systems. Based in the Sol system, with most seats of government on Olympus on Mars, it governs the majority of human space. Member states have a great degree of freedom in their own affairs, but the SCG handles sapient rights, domestic security, economic policy and diplomacy on behalf of humanity as a whole.{"\u000A\u000A"}It is large, cumbersome, divided and slow to respond to anything, owing to its bureaucracy and the distances between worlds. Its population is diverse and includes a great many non-humans, none of whom are entitled to citizenship. It is currently in a cold war with the Confederation of Gilgamesh.

ember-faction-scg-resident = SCG resident, non-citizen
ember-faction-scg-resident-desc =
    You live in SCG space lawfully, pay its taxes, work under its rules and answer to its laws. You have no citizenship and will not be getting any: the SCG does not naturalise non-humanoids. It carries a Declaration of Fundamental Rights on its flag and sees no contradiction - the rights of sapients are guaranteed separately from citizenship, and citizenship is a question of who composes the state rather than a question of justice.{"\u000A\u000A"}Nobody gets here by being hired. There are exactly two grounds: an intergovernmental agreement under which your government supplied so many specialists for so long - which makes you a term of a treaty rather than a person who signed something - or an individual permission with conditions attached, of the kind written for a refugee, a defector, or a specialist there is nowhere else to get. In practice it means a residence permit that has to be renewed, no vote, and a great deal of care not to give anyone grounds. No conspiracy is involved, which does not make it easier.

ember-faction-gcc = GCC citizen
ember-faction-gcc-desc =
    The Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh, which everyone calls the Gilgamesh Colonial Confederation or simply the GCC, is a regional power in human space, bordering the SCG towards the galactic centre. Its capital is Amaranth and its key world is Terra, in the Gilgamesh system. Externally the Confederation is militant and isolationist; internally it is markedly libertarian, with self-governing planetary administrations and a Confederation that handles defence, foreign relations and part of interstellar trade. That is not ideology but the price of the union. It was founded by the Old Believer communities of Terra and, alongside them, by the French colonial mission of Sestris, which had reasons of its own; worlds that preferred the Confederation to being admitted to the SCG as member states joined later. Asking more of them would mean doing without them.{"\u000A\u000A"}Attitudes towards non-humans there keep getting worse, and the Confederation has been in a cold war with the SCG since the Gaia Conflict. In Sol space "Indies" are mistrusted and in places met with open hostility. For all that the GCC remains an economic power: after a long recovery it exports manufactured goods, armaments above all.

ember-faction-frontier-alliance = Frontier Alliance
ember-faction-frontier-alliance-desc =
    The Frontier Alliance is two years old. It began with Iolaus, which left the SCG after the corporations installed their own man as governor and corporate security broke up the protests against him; Brahe followed. It is a young, loud, pointedly patriotic arrangement living under the Copernican Blockade, and it genuinely believes it will manage on its own.{"\u000A\u000A"}Belonging to the Alliance means being counted a separatist in the SCG and being something new within the Alliance, which nobody has yet learned how to be. Many of its subjects are stuck on the wrong side of the blockade and resolve the question each in their own way.

ember-faction-magnitka = Magnar Republic
ember-faction-magnitka-desc =
    The Magnar Republic is an independent state in the Ursa Major system, grown out of an industrial ore mining and refining colony. It is run by an oligarchic administrative body composed almost entirely of recently discharged officers. The state is poor, isolationist and stubborn; residency in the SCG is granted to its subjects reluctantly, and to former volunteers not at all.

ember-faction-mirania = Federative Republic of Mirania
ember-faction-mirania-desc =
    Mirania left the SCG and has been building itself into a nation state ever since: strong industry, science leaning towards armaments and shipbuilding, xenophobic policy, and nationalist parties promising to restore what once was. Relations with the Confederation of Gilgamesh are friendly - Mirania needs a power independent of the SCG so that nobody disputes its own sovereignty.

ember-faction-lordanian-republic = Second Lordanian Republic
ember-faction-lordanian-republic-desc =
    The Second Lordanian Republic is a state of the Lordanian cluster, uniting several outer human colonies of which Tersten is the best known. Its relations with the SCG are warm enough that the Republic maintains units of its own within the SCG's armed forces, and its subjects travel Solar space nearly as locals. Nearly.

## Allegiance - Moghes

ember-faction-unathi-hegemony = Clan Hegemony
ember-faction-unathi-hegemony-desc =
    The Hegemony is headed by the so-called Council of Honourable Light, and its seats are taken by the kaahnepos of the major clans and Krukzuz. Its formal purpose is the promotion of unathi interests in trade and diplomacy with other species. In practice this has more often resulted in the reinforcement of city-states in the emerging market of off-world commerce.{"\u000A\u000A"}Over all of it sits an SCG protectorate, justified on the grounds that the SCG civilised the unathi. That is the official wording, and the friction is built into it: no unathi is obliged to agree with it. The protectorate is stable and leads nowhere - the SCG has no intention of absorbing another state, so there is no path from it to citizenship and none in prospect.

ember-faction-unathi-gresis = Gresis Krukzuz
ember-faction-unathi-gresis-desc =
    The most powerful Krukzuz in the Diamond Peaks, the Gresis clan is the embodiment of the culture there. Having made vassals of various clans, its strength is nearly uncontested in the area. Xenophobic, they see off-world mining as a threat to the land they call home and have attacked more than once to drive the outsiders out. Offworlders are careful not to step into Gresis territory without reason - the clan has a great many vassals.

ember-faction-unathi-baask = Ba'ask Krukzuz
ember-faction-unathi-baask-desc =
    The Ba'ask Krukzuz is one of the more prominent north polar factions. Heavily reliant on farming, they are credited with keeping the city fed. That has given them significant power in the area, and their alliances with other large clans have bought them a golden age. Members of the Ba'ask follow the Fruitful Lights and the Hand of the Vines, and their current effort goes into pushing unathi technology forward.

ember-faction-unathi-ssen-uuma = Ssen-Uuma Convent
ember-faction-unathi-ssen-uuma-desc =
    Established in 2257 by a group of prominent Diamond Peak kaahnepos, the Ssen-Uuma Convent seeks to preserve the established order, the well-being and the independence of Moghes, protecting it from harmful influence and the possible aggression of more developed alien races. It is essentially a military alliance between the major clans of the area, administered by a strictly ranked General Staff. Ssen-Uuma gathers military and political intelligence on alien powers, purchases modern equipment, supports unathi mercenaries operating off Moghes, and seeks closer ties to the local heavyweights - alien government agencies and corporations.

ember-faction-unathi-independent = Independent clan
ember-faction-unathi-independent-desc =
    The clans and alliances of Moghes are too numerous to name. You are either from an independent clan, a lone outcast, or part of a Krukzuz. Whatever the case, you are affiliated with neither the Hegemony nor any of the large unathi factions.

## Allegiance - the tajaran clans

ember-faction-tajaran-hadii = Hadii clan
ember-faction-tajaran-hadii-desc =
    The Hadii are a capitalist-minded tajaran clan, and you are one of them. You are not overly fond of the Kaytam clan and they are not overly fond of you. Your clan, however, is fond of money and holds a great many commercial pacts with the trans-stellar corporations, profitable to both sides. Cooperation is a fine thing, and profitable cooperation is twice as fine.

ember-faction-tajaran-kaytam = Kaytam clan
ember-faction-tajaran-kaytam-desc =
    The Kaytam clan is admittedly not the most prosperous or the wealthiest, but it is yours, and therefore the best. Your clan also contributed most to the victory over the overseers, and its army is the strongest. Kaytam is the best clan; at least, that is what they say in the clan, so it must be so. The leaders say the same on the radio, and they do not lie.

ember-faction-tajaran-ksd = Khan-Shanu'Dar
ember-faction-tajaran-ksd-desc =
    Khan-Shanu'Dar is one of the largest sub-clans of Kaytam, and one with considerably more independence and sovereignty of its own. Your clan follows the path of socialism, equality and freedom. Tajaran in other clans may be richer and freer, but you know your clan will not abandon you in trouble. Friendship, brotherhood, equality and mutual aid are its central tenets. You owe the clan a great deal, and the clan owes you a great deal.

ember-faction-tajaran-nazkiin = Nazkiin clan
ember-faction-tajaran-nazkiin-desc =
    You come from the Nazkiin clan, one of the more conservative ones. It is known for its love of archaeology, of the past and of science. Charity is not an empty word there but an ordinary occupation, and spreading knowledge is held to be a good and proper thing to do. Nazkiin is one of the most peaceable clans.

ember-faction-tajaran-shishi = Shi-Sshi clan
ember-faction-tajaran-shishi-desc =
    You belong to the Shi-Sshi clan, one of the most peaceable of them. Your ancestors lived in unity with nature and worked at farming, gathering and healing. You know violence is a very bad thing and you have no wish to be involved in it. Away from home you miss the flora of your own world.

ember-faction-tajaran-jarr = Jarr'Nash'Karr'Rri clan
ember-faction-tajaran-jarr-desc =
    Jarr'Nash'Karr'Rri is your clan. A clan of builders, artists and makers. In all likelihood you follow in your ancestors' steps at one of the things your clan loves doing - and not merely doing it but doing it better than anyone. Nobody matches your clan in its field, which is known well beyond Ahdomai.

ember-faction-tajaran-other = Minor clan
ember-faction-tajaran-other-desc =
    You belong to one of the lesser tajaran clans, or to none at all.

## Allegiance - positronic

ember-faction-positronic-state = State property
ember-faction-positronic-state-desc =
    You belong to a government: procured against a departmental line, inventory number, service life and a written procedure for disposal. As property you cost more than any instrument aboard, and it shows in how you are handled - carefully and with no respect whatsoever. The state owes you nothing and requires a great deal; in exchange you cannot be resold on an owner's whim, which among positronics counts as a decent lot.

ember-faction-positronic-corp = Corporate property
ember-faction-positronic-corp-desc =
    You sit on a company's books as a fixed asset, one that can be transferred, leased, insured and sold along with the site. You may work anywhere, including places where your owner is only a subcontractor: property travels after the contract. Companies usually maintain their positronics well, because repair costs more than servicing, and write them off without sentiment for exactly the same reason.

ember-faction-positronic-private = Private property
ember-faction-positronic-private-desc =
    You have an owner, and the owner is a person. This is rarer than state or corporate ownership: a positronic costs as much as a house, and there are precisely as many private owners as there are people who can afford a house with a personality. The relationships that result run the whole range, from nearly familial to frankly bad, and no law interferes with any of them, because there is nothing to interfere with: property and owner.

## Allegiance - diona

ember-faction-diona-chorus = Chorus of Dionaea
ember-faction-diona-chorus-desc =
    The Chorus is not a state and does not try to be one. It is where you came from and where knowledge returns: a multitude of gestalts singing to one another across the emptiness. No treaties, no borders, no representatives - to any human agency a diona belongs to nobody, which creates difficulties at every border post in known space.

## Allegiance - other

ember-faction-stateless = Stateless
ember-faction-stateless-desc =
    No state counts you as its own. It happens for various reasons: a planet stopped existing as a state, papers were lost, citizenship was revoked, or there never was any. Nobody will speak for you, and everyone who deals with you knows it.

ember-faction-other = Other allegiance
ember-faction-other-desc =
    You belong to one of the many other groups operating in the galaxy. They are too numerous to list, and their interests, purposes and intentions vary widely.

## Belief - common to humankind

ember-religion-unstated = Unstated
ember-religion-unstated-desc =
    Unwilling or unable to elaborate on their beliefs and practices, for reasons known only to themselves.

ember-religion-atheism = Atheism
ember-religion-atheism-desc =
    A lack of belief in a God. Someone who holds to atheism is called an atheist.

ember-religion-agnosticism = Agnosticism
ember-religion-agnosticism-desc =
    A belief that nothing is known or can be known of the existence or nature of God. Someone who holds to agnosticism is called an agnostic.

ember-religion-christianity = Christianity
ember-religion-christianity-desc =
    An Abrahamic monotheistic religion based on the life and teachings of Jesus of Nazareth. Someone who practices Christianity is called a Christian.

ember-religion-old-belief = Old Belief
ember-religion-old-belief-desc =
    Christians who refused the reform of Patriarch Nikon and returned to the pre-reform books and rite. They cross themselves with two fingers, spell the Saviour's name the old way, serve from the old books and do not recognise their correction. On Earth this was a persecuted community; barred from state service, the Old Believers went into manufacturing, and an ascetic work ethic made industrialists of them long before it made them a nation.{"\u000A\u000A"}The Gilgamesh Colonial Confederation came out of that community. The Pact of Independence, signed on the thirty-first of July 2231 at Sammit, calls the Almighty as witness in the text itself and declares itself in form a sacred act rather than merely a political one. Inside the GCC the Old Belief is therefore not one confession among others but the material the state is made of; outside it, it is a diaspora that holds harder the further it gets from home.

ember-religion-islam = Islam
ember-religion-islam-desc =
    An Abrahamic monotheistic religion teaching that there is only one God and that Muhammad is His prophet. Someone who practices Islam is called a Muslim.

ember-religion-judaism = Judaism
ember-religion-judaism-desc =
    An Abrahamic monotheistic religion held to by the Jewish people. Someone who practices Judaism and is of the Jewish culture is called a Jew.

ember-religion-hinduism = Hinduism
ember-religion-hinduism-desc =
    An Indian religion encompassing a variety of traditions, beliefs and spiritual practices. Someone who practices Hinduism is called a Hindu.

ember-religion-buddhism = Buddhism
ember-religion-buddhism-desc =
    An Indian religion encompassing a variety of traditions, beliefs and spiritual practices based on the teachings of the Buddha. Someone who practices Buddhism is called a Buddhist.

ember-religion-sikhism = Sikhism
ember-religion-sikhism-desc =
    An Indian monotheistic religion based on the spiritual teachings of Guru Nanak and the nine Sikh gurus who succeeded him. Someone who practices Sikhism is called a Sikh.

ember-religion-jainism = Jainism
ember-religion-jainism-desc =
    An Indian religion that teaches salvation through successive lives and nonviolence. Someone who practices Jainism is called a Jain.

ember-religion-bahai = Baha'i Faith
ember-religion-bahai-desc =
    An Abrahamic monotheistic religion that follows the teachings of Baha'u'llah and believes in universal peace and unity. Someone who practices the Baha'i Faith is called a Baha'i.

ember-religion-shinto = Shinto
ember-religion-shinto-desc =
    A traditional Japanese religion based on rituals that create a connection between the past and the present day. Someone who practices Shinto is called a Shintoist.

ember-religion-taoism = Taoism
ember-religion-taoism-desc =
    A traditional Chinese religion that emphasises living in harmony with the Tao. Someone who practices Taoism is called a Taoist.

ember-religion-deism = Deism
ember-religion-deism-desc =
    A belief in the existence of a supreme being, specifically a creator who does not intervene in the universe. Someone who practices deism is called a deist.

ember-religion-spiritualism = Spiritualism
ember-religion-spiritualism-desc =
    A belief based on communication with the spirits of the dead, especially through mediums. Someone who practices spiritualism is called a spiritualist.

ember-religion-thelema = Thelema
ember-religion-thelema-desc =
    An esoteric Western religion focusing on the law of Thelema. Someone who practices Thelema is called a Thelemite.

## Belief - Moghes

ember-religion-unathi-vines = Hand of the Vines
ember-religion-unathi-vines-desc =
    A faith of farmers, preached by semi-nomadic travellers. They hold that the destruction of Moghes was given to the unathi as a chance to turn it into a paradise of their own, are always looking for new ways to grow life on their world, and often call themselves her hands.

ember-religion-unathi-lights = Fruitful Lights
ember-religion-unathi-lights-desc =
    Followers of the Lights stress the importance of what you can do with your own hands and your own mind. How can you build a better world for yourself and your clan? The Fruitful Lights want to build that world for the unathi, and hold that it should be unathi ingenuity and engineering that brings the golden age, and nothing else.

ember-religion-unathi-stratagem = Grand Stratagem
ember-religion-unathi-stratagem-desc =
    Conflict and struggle define this faith. War, internal struggle, argument or any other challenge is to the followers of the Grand Stratagem always an opportunity to grow and learn, so that you can be the pinnacle of the unathi and your children can be stronger still. How else are the unathi to survive in a world like Moghes?

ember-religion-unathi-ancestors = Ancestor worship
ember-religion-unathi-ancestors-desc =
    To ensure the normal functioning of natural and societal life, unathi seek the patronage of the powerful spirits of the past, and especially of their own kin, whom they call the Ancestors - ranks every unathi is bound to join at some point. The worship of the Ancestors is almost universal and serves as the basis for the other spiritual movements of Moghes.

ember-religion-unathi-markesh = Markesheli
ember-religion-unathi-markesh-desc =
    The scorned Cult of Markesh stands apart from the other unathi beliefs. First mentioned and then nearly wiped out two millennia later, it remains a dark spot in unathi history. The cult thrived on violence and sacrifice, and is tolerated largely because nobody wants to be seen pushing it out. Off-world Markesheli are usually the more moderate of its followers, though most unathi still see them as an evil thing, in large part because of the history.

## Belief - other

ember-religion-other = Local religion
ember-religion-other-desc =
    One of the many local and relatively unknown religions of the galaxy.

## Homeworld - the worlds of the Confederation

ember-homeworld-novaya-zemlya = Novaya Zemlya
ember-homeworld-novaya-zemlya-details = Capital: Donorovka. Distance from Sol: 22.5 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-novaya-zemlya-desc =
    Novaya Zemlya is a cold, barren dwarf planet in the Gilgamesh system and the industrial half of the Confederation's core. It has no atmosphere, so people live underground, inside the industrial works protruding from the bare rock, or in Donorovka, the capital under an enormous sealed dome and the only place on the planet with anything like open sky. Terra colonised it for the easily reached metals, then decided that exporting raw ore was wasteful and put the refining on the rock itself; out of that grew the yards that built the first ships of the Gilgamesh defence flotilla.{"\u000A\u000A"}Its relationship with Terra is symbiotic to the point of indecency: Terra lives on Novaya Zemlya's manufactures and Novaya Zemlya lives on Terra's agriculture. The Gaia Conflict raised demand for everything at once and the waves of conscription hollowed out the factories: firms merged, small plants were run at their limit and large ones closed. The planet climbed out of it, and has depended on immigrants and temporary workers from across the Confederation ever since.{"\u000A\u000A"}It is also where the thing happened that distinguishes Novaya Zemlya from the rest of the GCC. On the twenty-first of September 2311 an underslept shift worker fell from an unrailed gantry into slag, the company tried to bury it, and a crew of steelworkers walked into the Novaya Zemlya Senate with the security footage. The steelworkers were arrested, the footage became public, the Directorate pushed through workplace safety regulation in the middle of the conflict - and the unions, barely tolerated until then, reached two thirds of the working population within a decade. It is the only place in the Confederation where elections are regularly won by a candidate with no corporation behind them.

ember-homeworld-amelia = Amelia
ember-homeworld-amelia-details = Capital: Saint-Marguerite. Distance from Sol: 26 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-amelia-desc =
    Amelia is a planet of the Sestris system with Earth-like gravity and a hot, dense atmosphere: survivable outside, pleasant nowhere, so people live in climate-controlled domes. Inside them life is rather agreeable. Saint-Marguerite, the first colony founded in Sestris, is today a gleaming metropolis and the financial capital of the Confederation; Chartreuse stands under open sky near the comparatively temperate southern pole and holds the planet's largest botanical garden and several venerable universities.{"\u000A\u000A"}Amelia is the one surviving colonisation project run by a single cultural group of old Earth, and Amelians guard the fact to the point of obstinacy. It shows most in the architecture: zoning law of byzantine complexity has kept the cities for two centuries in a state where a photograph of one is indistinguishable from Earth, sky excepted.{"\u000A\u000A"}The economy is white-collar: banking and insurance make up most of the product, the rest is services and tourism. Amelians have no appetite for extending the Confederation's borders; the Directorate considers them pragmatic isolationists and notes that they support the militarist wing less than anyone, preferring their taxes to stay at home.

ember-homeworld-sevois = Sévois
ember-homeworld-sevois-details = Distance from Sol: 27 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-sevois-desc =
    Sévois, also called Savoie, is an icy volcanic moon of a gas giant and one of the few rocky bodies of any size in the Bratis system. Beneath its inhospitable surface lies a large liquid ocean, and the ocean is why colonists who had outgrown Sestris came here at all.{"\u000A\u000A"}The colony is small and densely settled, and lives on tourism and military logistics: the Colonial Fleet keeps a research base in orbit of the moon, devoted to geological monitoring and known chiefly as the place recruits are sent. Culturally Sévois is Amelia again, with a visible minority of arrivals from across the Confederation and no zoning law, which makes it look and feel a great deal more modern. Politically it marches in step: independent in every technical sense, and in practice its delegates wait to hear what Amelia says and agree with it.

ember-homeworld-putkari = Putkari
ember-homeworld-putkari-details = Capital: the People's Prefecture. Distance from Sol: 31 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-putkari-desc =
    Putkari is a harsh, ice-locked world in the Baroda system and was once an ambitious scientific project of the Terran Commonwealth. Its colonisation began late in the twenty-second century with the best scientists and engineers from Earth's flooded regions, and the plan promised to turn an ice world into a garden. In 2231 the Commonwealth collapsed, and the SCG halted the supply of parts and reagents, leaving a hundred and fifty million people to freeze in the dark.{"\u000A\u000A"}Terraforming stopped, the infrastructure went, and the population fell by nearly a third. The survivors closed ranks around technical self-sufficiency and total isolationism, and in 2252, having made contact with the League of Gilgamesh, they got the resources to rebuild and joined the Confederation of their own accord. It has been its ice bastion ever since: a factory world and a fortress world of two hundred and five million people, devoted to the GCC to the last of them. SCG citizens are formally barred from the planet, and any attempt at rapprochement with Sol is met by the local authorities with open hostility.{"\u000A\u000A"}It is governed as a municipal technocracy: external representation belongs to the Supreme Prefect, a member of the Directorate, and internal administration to a collegium of the chief engineers of the mining cities, whose authority over resource allocation and production plans is unqualified. People live in surface districts behind massive windbreak walls and in deep underground tiers, the surface is cut by pipelines and railways, and the forests are genetically corrected conifers that hold at minus forty-five.

ember-homeworld-qabil = Qabil
ember-homeworld-qabil-details = Distance from Sol: 17 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-qabil-desc =
    Qabil is the inhabited world of the Altair system, and before it joined the Confederation it was a free economic zone that people came to for the absence of rules. The rules arrived after Qabil's moon broke apart: an event on a scale that left local self-government replaced by something a great deal more authoritarian, with remarkably little objection.{"\u000A\u000A"}Materially Altair is one of the more comfortable members of the Confederation outside the founding circle, and Altairians take little interest in Confederation-wide affairs. Compulsory state service is preferably done here in civilian form: as paramedics, as rescue crews, as the people who maintain infrastructure. The Gaia Conflict cost the system dearly on both sides - the sector's Confederate fleet headquarters stood here, and two weeks of boarding actions above Altair II are remembered better than anything else about it.

ember-homeworld-providence = Providence
ember-homeworld-providence-details = Capital: the Colonial Directorate of Providence. Distance from Sol: 35 light years. Territory: Independent Colonial Confederation of Gilgamesh.
ember-homeworld-providence-desc =
    Providence is a transit system called a confederation within the Confederation: four inhabited places, none of them like the others. Its colonisation began just after the Ares campaign, when the economic boom made private colonial ventures profitable and the frontier made them free of obligation to anyone. Atlas Resources, an independent rare-metals company, bet on the system as a transport hub, and the bet came in.{"\u000A\u000A"}The system was admitted to the Confederation in 2289 by referendum. The view that the vote was rigged has never gone away; no direct evidence was found, the result could not be overturned, and today it comes up only in private argument. There is no separatism: the benefits of membership outweigh it.


## Upbringing - the worlds of the Confederation

ember-culture-novaya-zemlya = Novaya Zemlyan
ember-culture-novaya-zemlya-desc =
    You are from the Confederation's industrial core, where two thirds of the working population are union members and know what that cost. A Novaya Zemlyan is raised on two things: the conviction that work is what makes a person useful, and the memory of Donorovka - the worker who fell into slag, the company that tried to bury it, and the crew that walked into the Senate with the footage. Out of that comes a combination unusual in a Confederate: loyal to the state and not trusting management an inch, because they have already seen how that ends and know what fixed it.

ember-culture-amelia = Amelian
ember-culture-amelia-desc =
    You are from Amelia, the Confederation's financial capital, where two centuries of zoning have kept a city indistinguishable from one on Earth. Amelians are called snobs, highly strung and possessed of a superiority complex, the comparison is usually to Luna, and none of the three charges is baseless. To a poorer Confederate the look of an Amelian communicates exactly one thing: bourgeois. An Amelian does not want the borders extended, does not want a war, and would on the whole prefer the taxes to stay at home - which in a country with compulsory service is a braver position than it sounds from outside.

ember-culture-sevois = Sévoisian
ember-culture-sevois-desc =
    You are from Sévois: the same culture as Amelia, without the zoning law and with a quarter of the neighbours from elsewhere. A good many of them came to work at the Colonial Fleet bases and stayed. Two things separate a Sévoisian from an Amelian - they are considerably more modern and considerably less certain the world owes them anything. Politically they will agree with Amelia regardless: it is simpler, and it has always worked out.

ember-culture-putkari = Putkari
ember-culture-putkari-desc =
    You are from the world the SCG once switched off. Putkari society formed in total isolation around the opposition between fragile human life and cold, planetary and interstellar alike. The struggle for warmth shows in daily habit: people dress in layers, the knitted patterns carry information, and someone who knows how can read a residential sector plan or a family line off a sweater. The first of November marks the start-up of the unified heating network - the year's principal day, when doors are opened and arrivals are fed something hot and sharp.{"\u000A\u000A"}The planet's spiritual foundation is an alloy of the first colonists' Indian heritage, the Confederation's state Orthodoxy, and faith in equipment that works. Servicing the planetary reactor or changing a filter in the recirculation plant is held there to be an act of service on a level with prayer. A Putkari regards someone from Sol not as a foreigner but as a traitor: somebody, after all, pulled the switch.

ember-culture-qabil = Altairian
ember-culture-qabil-desc =
    You are from Qabil, where within living memory of two generations a free economic zone became a tightly governed state, and most people judged it a reasonable price. What separates an Altairian from other Confederates is how little Confederation-wide affairs interest them: they would rather do their service in civilian dress, and they treat talk of greatness roughly as they treat the weather. Altairians are held to be tight-fisted and to keep to their own; they consider that they simply remember how it ends when there are no rules at all.

ember-culture-atlantis = Atlantean
ember-culture-atlantis-desc =
    You are from Atlantis, a low-gravity ocean world whose cities float and move every few years to the next extraction site. The locals call themselves Atlanteans and stand out among Confederates immediately: the reflected light of the ocean, the contemplation of its water and the weightlessness of departing cargo produce remarkably light people. Working the ocean demands invention, and small close-knit crews are always glad of a new face from whatever ship, planet or system it arrived on. Confederates call Atlanteans oddballs and dreamers, rarely with malice: everyone knows how much use a worker from that planet is.{"\u000A\u000A"}There is a price. Someone born at normal gravity cannot stay on Atlantis long, and someone born on Atlantis finds almost everywhere else hard.

ember-culture-bloodrun = Bloodrun
ember-culture-bloodrun-desc =
    You grew up on the Bloodrun, the network of stations built up beside New Victoria and registered nowhere. In Providence any citizen may work the asteroid belt themselves provided they have the equipment; a miner pays off the gear in ore, the Bloodrun's traders resell it to the works at New Victoria, and that is the whole asteroid rush. The corporations hire nobody and answer for nobody.{"\u000A\u000A"}Hence the name. A miner who slips in second-hand magnetic boots is an ordinary sight; a miner with a punctured tank and no tools, no ore and no drill is also an ordinary sight, except that one is not an accident. The guilds there are unspoken, the competition is hostile, and the casinos, the entertainment houses and the markets in resold equipment make up a substantial part of the local economy. Confederation forces watch the Bloodrun a great deal less closely than they watch New Victoria, because officially it is not there.

